From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] firmware: arm_scmi: perf: dedupe OPPs by frequency when populating a device

On Sky1 (O6N) the Mali GPU power domain (ACPI HID CIXH5000) is an SCMI
performance-domain genpd. When the GPU device attaches, scmi_pd_attach_dev()
calls perf_ops->device_opps_add() -> scmi_dvfs_device_opps_add(), which
walks the firmware DVFS level table and registers one OPP per level via
dev_pm_opp_add_dynamic().

The CIX firmware perf table for CIXH5000 exposes two distinct performance
levels that resolve to the same frequency (800 MHz). The existing
parse-time dedup only rejects duplicate perf *levels* (xa_insert on
opp->perf), not duplicate *frequencies*, so both survive and are added as
OPPs with identical freq. The OPP core then logs, at boot:

  genpd genpd:0:CIXH5000:00: _opp_is_duplicate: duplicate OPPs detected.
    Existing: freq: 800000000 ... New: freq: 800000000 ...

dev_pm_opp_add_dynamic() does not fail on a duplicate (it returns 0), so
the only effect is boot log noise; the second same-freq level is redundant.

Root cause: a firmware DVFS table advertising two levels at one frequency,
added verbatim by the SCMI OPP populate path. Fixed by skipping a
frequency that an earlier level in the same table already contributed,
before calling dev_pm_opp_add_dynamic(). SCMI level_set/get use the
protocol's own opps_by_lvl/opps_by_freq lookup tables, not the OPP table,
so DVFS operation is unaffected. On well-formed firmware tables the guard
is a no-op, so the change is safe for all SCMI platforms.

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/perf.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/perf.c
@@ -864,7 +864,7 @@
 static int scmi_dvfs_device_opps_add(const struct scmi_protocol_handle *ph,
 				     struct device *dev, u32 domain)
 {
-	int idx, ret;
+	int idx, j, ret;
 	unsigned long freq;
 	struct dev_pm_opp_data data = {};
 	struct perf_dom_info *dom;
@@ -879,6 +879,24 @@
 		else
 			freq = dom->opp[idx].indicative_freq * dom->mult_factor;

+		/*
+		 * Some firmware DVFS tables expose several performance levels
+		 * that resolve to the same frequency (e.g. the CIX Sky1 GPU
+		 * perf domain reports 800 MHz twice). Registering both makes
+		 * the OPP core log a spurious "duplicate OPPs detected"
+		 * warning, so skip a frequency an earlier level already added.
+		 */
+		for (j = 0; j < idx; j++) {
+			unsigned long prev = !dom->level_indexing_mode ?
+				dom->opp[j].perf * dom->mult_factor :
+				dom->opp[j].indicative_freq * dom->mult_factor;
+
+			if (prev == freq)
+				break;
+		}
+		if (j < idx)
+			continue;
+
 		/* All OPPs above the sustained frequency are treated as turbo */
 		data.turbo = freq > dom->sustained_freq_khz * 1000UL;

